K1 limits of D: A_rel = h . n.nabla(u_b/gamma), and D': A_rel = A - h . a_b
  clock acceleration A (static clock, exact) = [0, -Derivative(N(x, y), x)/(B(x, y)**2*N(x, y)), -Derivative(N(x, y), y)/(B(x, y)**2*N(x, y))]
  (i)  u_b = n (comoving):                   D - A = [0, 0, 0]
  (ii) static clock, ANY steady baryon flow: D - A = [0, 0, 0]
  D' with baryons held at rest beside a static clock: A - h.a_b = [0, 0, 0]  (the law is OFF in any held medium)
  (iii) clock moving with velocity e V(T,x,y), baryons at rest, Phi = e^2 phi:
        D^i to O(e^2) minus e^2 d_i phi = [-2*e**2*Derivative(phi(x, y), x), -2*e**2*Derivative(phi(x, y), y)]
        D' = A - h.a_b: (h.a_b)^i to O(e^2) minus e^2 d_i phi = [-2*e**2*Derivative(phi(x, y), x), -2*e**2*Derivative(phi(x, y), y)]  (free-falling clock, A = 0: D' = -grad Phi)
  (d) flat space, static clock, baryon flow e vb(T,x,y) along x: D^x = e**2*Derivative(vb(T, x, y), T)  (exact: d_t of the flow)
  K1: (i) True, (ii) True, (iii) D False, (iii) D' False, flat d_t True -> FAIL

K2 matter kinetic structure. About a background with A_rel = y0 (u = |y0|/a0) and baryons at rest, a uniform
   displacement xi(t) changes A_rel by xi'' (K1(d); D' likewise through a_b). P has no time derivative in (A),
   so at gamma_L = 0 P is eliminated: L_matter = rho xi'^2/2 + J(|y0 + c xi''|) - k xi^2/2 (c = 1; M2: c = 0).
  Hessian of J in xi'' : along A_rel 1/(K*(u + 1)**2) ; across A_rel 1/(K*(u + 1)) ; off-diagonal 0
  linearized Euler-Lagrange: -k*xi(t) - rho*Derivative(xi(t), (t, 2)) + Derivative(xi(t), (t, 4))/K = 0
  characteristic polynomial in s (xi ~ e^(s t)): (-K*k - K*rho*s**2 + s**4)/K;  s^2 roots: [-sqrt(K)*sqrt(K*rho**2 + 4*k)/2 + K*rho/2, sqrt(K)*sqrt(K*rho**2 + 4*k)/2 + K*rho/2]
  Ostrogradsky Hamiltonian (q1 = xi, q2 = xi', p2 = H xi''): H_O = K*p2**2/2 + k*q1**2/2 + p1*q2 - q2**2*rho/2;  d^2 H_O/dp1^2 = 0  -> linear in p1, unbounded below when the H xi''^2 term is present (H != 0)

